## Camera vs photo library: two sources, one result
The user can give you a photo *two ways*: take a **new one with the camera**, or **pick an existing one from their library**. expo-image-picker handles *both* with nearly identical APIs, and a good app usually offers *both* (an action sheet: "Take Photo" / "Choose from Library"). Understanding the small differences, and the shared permission/result shape, is today's job.

## The two calls
```tsx
import * as ImagePicker from 'expo-image-picker';

// 1) Take a NEW photo with the camera
const camResult = await ImagePicker.launchCameraAsync({
  mediaTypes: ImagePicker.MediaTypeOptions.Images,
  quality: 0.7,          // 0..1 - compress at capture, see D5
  allowsEditing: true,   // lets the user crop before returning
});

// 2) Pick an EXISTING photo from the library
const libResult = await ImagePicker.launchImageLibraryAsync({
  mediaTypes: ImagePicker.MediaTypeOptions.Images,
  quality: 0.7,
});
```
*Both* return the *same result shape*: `{ canceled: boolean, assets: [{ uri, width, height, fileSize, ... }] }`. So your handling code is shared, the *only* real difference is which function you call and which *permission* it needs.

## Permissions: ask at the right moment, handle "no"
Camera and library access are *separate permissions*, and the *#1 production bug* here is assuming permission is granted:
```tsx
const { status } = await ImagePicker.requestCameraPermissionsAsync();
if (status !== 'granted') {
  // The user said NO. Do NOT crash or silently fail.
  // Explain why you need it and offer a path to Settings.
  return showPermissionExplainer();
}
```
*Ask in context* (when the user taps "Add photo"), not on app launch, an out-of-context permission prompt gets denied far more often. And *always* design the *denied* path: a calm explanation plus a button that deep-links to the OS settings (`Linking.openSettings()`), because once denied, you *cannot* re-prompt; only the user can change it in Settings.

## Handle cancellation, always
The user *will* tap "Cancel". `result.canceled === true` is a *normal* outcome, not an error, guard for it before touching `result.assets`, or you'll crash on an undefined.

