## expo-notifications: receiving, handling, and channels
With a token in hand, today you handle notifications *on the device*: what happens when one arrives in the foreground vs background, what happens when the user *taps* it, and how Android **channels** give users granular control. This client-side handling is what makes notifications feel polished rather than janky.

## Foreground vs background arrival
A subtle but important behaviour: by default, a push that arrives *while your app is open* is *not* shown as a banner, the OS assumes you'll handle it in-app. You decide:
```tsx
Notifications.setNotificationHandler({
  handleNotification: async () => ({
    shouldShowBanner: true,  // show the banner even in foreground
    shouldPlaySound: true,
    shouldSetBadge: true,
  }),
});
```
*Background/closed* arrivals are shown by the OS automatically. Knowing this split explains the classic "it works when the app is closed but not when it's open" confusion.

## Responding to a tap (deep-linking into context)
The *point* of a notification is usually to take the user *somewhere*:
```tsx
const sub = Notifications.addNotificationResponseReceivedListener(res => {
  const data = res.notification.request.content.data; // your custom payload
  // e.g. navigate to the relevant screen
  router.push(`/logs/${data.logId}`);
});
```
*A notification that opens the app to a generic home screen wastes the tap.* Always carry `data` in the payload and route to the *specific* relevant screen, the friend's message, the log, the goal.

## Android notification channels (mandatory, and great UX)
Android requires every notification to belong to a **channel**, a category the *user* can independently control (sound, importance, on/off):
```tsx
await Notifications.setNotificationChannelAsync('reminders', {
  name: 'Hydration reminders',
  importance: Notifications.AndroidImportance.DEFAULT,
});
```
Channels are *pro-user*: instead of "all notifications on/off", the user can keep "reminders" but mute "tips". *Design a few meaningful channels* (reminders, achievements, tips) so users can tune you instead of muting you entirely, this directly *reduces* the chance they disable you.

