## Authentication in requests: tokens, refresh, and the 401 dance
Most APIs require *authentication*, so almost every request your app makes carries a credential, and handling that *correctly* on a flaky mobile network, especially the *token refresh* flow, is where a lot of subtle bugs live. (You'll do full mobile auth, biometrics, OAuth, in W14; today is specifically the *networking* side: attaching and refreshing tokens.)

## Attaching the token to every request
The clean way is an **axios interceptor** that adds the auth header automatically, so individual calls stay simple:
```tsx
api.interceptors.request.use(async (config) => {
  const token = await getAccessToken();        // from secure storage (W14)
  if (token) config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  return config;
});
```
*One place* attaches the token to *all* requests, no per-call boilerplate, no forgotten headers.

## The refresh dance (the part that's tricky)
Access tokens are *short-lived* (e.g. 15 min) for security; a *refresh token* (long-lived) gets you a new one. When a request returns **401 Unauthorized**, you transparently refresh and retry:
```tsx
api.interceptors.response.use(undefined, async (error) => {
  if (error.response?.status === 401 && !error.config._retried) {
    error.config._retried = true;
    await refreshAccessToken();         // use refresh token -> new access token
    return api(error.config);           // replay the original request
  }
  throw error;
});
```
The *non-obvious bugs* this must solve:
```text
- CONCURRENT 401s: 5 requests fail at once -> you must refresh ONCE, not 5 times.
  Queue the failed requests behind a single in-flight refresh, then replay all.
- REFRESH FAILS (refresh token expired/revoked) -> log the user out cleanly,
  don't loop forever retrying.
- INFINITE LOOP guard: mark requests as retried (_retried) so a persistent 401
  doesn't retry endlessly.
```
*Single-flight refresh* (one refresh shared by all queued requests) is the detail most naive implementations get wrong, and it causes race conditions and duplicate refreshes that log users out randomly.

## Mobile-specific care
On mobile, *store tokens securely* (Keychain/Keystore via expo-secure-store, W14, never AsyncStorage for tokens), and remember requests can be *interrupted mid-refresh* by a dropped connection, so the refresh flow itself needs the same resilience (timeout, retry) as any request.

